description:
extractLinks (src/parse.js:44) runs a global backtracking regex. Its target group `[^\]|#
]+` admits '[', and its heading group `#[^\]|
]*` and alias group `\|[^\]
]*` admit '[' as well. Every '[[' on one long line starts a match attempt that scans to the end of the line, fails to find ']]', and backtracks. Work is O(L^2) per line, and no note-size, line-length, or link-count cap runs before parsing. In the audit sandbox (Node 26, 1 CPU), the retained verify-19 transcripts show extractLinks on a single line of '[' taking 3.6-5.6 ms at 2 KB, 13.5-25.9 ms at 4 KB, 51-81 ms at 8 KB, 204-227 ms at 16 KB and 784 ms at 32 KB, about 3.5-4x per doubling. parseNote on the same body with frontmatter took 3.5, 13.2, 49.7, 193 and 778 ms from 2 KB to 32 KB, 3.75-4.0x per doubling. Two controls stayed near-linear: a benign '[[a]]' body took 0.23 ms at 32 KB, and the same '[' bytes split into 200-character lines took 6.2 ms. Extrapolating the quadratic gives about 3 s at 64 KiB, about 50 s at 256 KiB and about 13 min at 1 MiB. All of these sizes are under the 2 MB D1 row limit. Any managed key with write role can commit such a note inside its own prefix through update_note, save_session or save, as can anyone with push access to the repo. putFile commits to git before upsertLocal parses, so the note persists even if that request is killed. Three paths then re-parse it with no per-note budget: fullReindex parses every tar entry in one invocation before its DELETE batch (nightly cron and /admin/reindex), the webhook handler re-parses changed paths in waitUntil, and upsertLocal re-parses on every app or MCP write. Killing fullReindex leaves the D1 index stale but intact, because the parse loop runs before the deletes. It also skips the cron's hygiene snapshot and telemetry rollup. Other notes in the same webhook batch go unindexed. Damage is to the shared index (search, graph, backlinks, hygiene for every principal) and to the operator's Worker CPU spend. It lasts until the owner deletes the note. Whether this crosses the kill threshold depends on the deployed CPU limit, which wrangler.jsonc does not set.

claimed_root_cause:
All three character classes in /\[\[([^\]|#
]+)(?:#[^\]|
]*)?(?:\|[^\]
]*)?\]\]/g accept '[', so each '[[' start position on a line rescans to the end of that line. Nothing caps body size, line length or match count before extractLinks runs from parseNote (src/parse.js:104). Changing only the first group is not enough: the same sandbox showed the hunter's group-1-only fix staying quadratic on '[[a#' and '[[a|' repeated (38-56 ms at 16 KB, about 4x per doubling). Excluding '[' from all three groups stayed flat (0.01-0.04 ms at 16 KB) and returned the same link set on a sample.

